"Transforms each multivariate record into a Fourier-series curve so similar records tend to produce similar curve shapes.\n\nUse when: Several numeric variables form records whose overall similarities need exploratory inspection. The audience can be told how scaling and variable order affect the display.\n\nAvoid when: Interpretation requires reading original variable values directly from the plotted curves.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes an artificial curve parameter rather than time. Curve height is a weighted sum of fixed Fourier basis functions, using record values as coefficients.\n\nBuild: Select and standardize numeric variables using a stated policy. Assign variables to the conventional Fourier basis in a fixed order. Evaluate each record’s curve over a shared parameter range and color only meaningful groups.\n\nCheck: Check every curve uses the same variable order and normalization. Verify the basis formula and parameter range are documented. Compare apparent groups against original-variable plots before drawing conclusions.\n\nPitfalls: Curve crossings are intrinsic to this basis expansion and do not denote record events. Prefer a heatmap or parallel coordinates when the audience needs interpretable variable-specific differences.\n\nExample: Inspect synthetic flower measurements as Andrews curves by known species, while noting variable order and standardization.\n\nAlternatives: Parallel coordinates, Scatterplot matrix, Heatmap."
